{
  "id": "02-reverse-polish-notation",
  "title": "Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation",
  "track": "Stacks",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "topics": [
    "stacks",
    "arrays"
  ],
  "source": "canonical Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation concept; description, examples, and tests re-authored",
  "description": "The input is an array of tokens forming an arithmetic expression written in reverse Polish (postfix) notation. Tokens are integer literals or the operators \"+\", \"-\", \"*\" and \"/\". Division truncates toward zero. Return the value of the expression. The expression is always valid and never divides by zero.",
  "constraints": [
    "1 <= len(tokens) <= 5000",
    "integer tokens are between -10000 and 10000",
    "every intermediate result fits in a 32-bit signed integer",
    "the expression is always valid and never divides by zero"
  ],
  "parameters": [
    "tokens"
  ],
  "functionName": "eval_rpn",
  "starterCode": "def eval_rpn(tokens):\n    # Return the value of the postfix expression.\n    ...\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": {
        "tokens": [
          "4",
          "13",
          "5",
          "/",
          "+"
        ]
      },
      "output": 6,
      "explanation": "13 / 5 truncates to 2, then 4 + 2 = 6."
    },
    {
      "input": {
        "tokens": [
          "7",
          "-2",
          "/"
        ]
      },
      "output": -3,
      "explanation": "7 / -2 is -3.5 in exact arithmetic and truncates toward zero to -3."
    }
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Postfix means every operator applies to the two values sitting just below it. What structure holds \"the values just below\" naturally?",
    "Scan tokens left to right: numbers go into a waiting pile; each operator takes the top two, applies, and puts one value back.",
    "Push integer tokens. On an operator pop b then pop a, compute a op b (division truncates toward zero, so use int(a / b) or an explicit sign fix on //), and push the result. The last value standing is the answer."
  ],
  "solution": "One pass with a stack: push integer tokens, and on each operator pop the top two operands (right operand first), apply the operator with division truncating toward zero, and push the result. The final stack holds one value: O(n) time, O(n) space.",
  "solutionCode": "def eval_rpn(tokens):\n    def divide(a, b):\n        q = abs(a) // abs(b)\n        return -q if (a < 0) != (b < 0) else q\n    ops = {\"+\": lambda a, b: a + b, \"-\": lambda a, b: a - b, \"*\": lambda a, b: a * b, \"/\": divide}\n    stack = []\n    for token in tokens:\n        if token in ops:\n            b = stack.pop()\n            a = stack.pop()\n            stack.append(ops[token](a, b))\n        else:\n            stack.append(int(token))\n    return stack[0]\n",
  "bruteForceCode": "def eval_rpn(tokens):\n    def divide(a, b):\n        q = abs(a) // abs(b)\n        return -q if (a < 0) != (b < 0) else q\n    ops = {\"+\", \"-\", \"*\", \"/\"}\n    current = list(tokens)\n    while len(current) > 1:\n        idx = 0\n        while current[idx] not in ops:\n            idx += 1\n        a = int(current[idx - 2])\n        b = int(current[idx - 1])\n        op = current[idx]\n        if op == \"+\":\n            value = a + b\n        elif op == \"-\":\n            value = a - b\n        elif op == \"*\":\n            value = a * b\n        else:\n            value = divide(a, b)\n        current = current[:idx - 2] + [str(value)] + current[idx + 1:]\n    return int(current[0])\n",
  "failureHint": "hidden tests include a single number, division truncation on negatives, and long chains",
  "expectedComplexity": {
    "time": "O(n)",
    "space": "O(n)"
  },
